{title: Lo, How a Rose E'er Blooming}
{artist: German carol · tr. Theodore Baker}
{key: F}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: 69}

Verse 1
[F]Lo, how a rose e'er [C]blooming from [Bb]tender [C]stem hath [F]sprung,
of Jesse's lineage [C]coming as [Bb]men of [C]old have [F]sung.
It [Dm]came, a floweret [C]bright,
a[F]mid the cold of [C]winter, when [Bb]half spent [C]was the [F]night.

Verse 2
[F]Isaiah 'twas foretold it, the [C]Rose [Bb]I [C]have in [F]mind;
With Mary we [C]behold it, [Bb]the virgin [C]mother kind. [F]To
show [Dm]God's love aright, [C]she
bore to men a [C]Savior, When [Bb]half spent [C]was the [F]night.

Verse 3
[F]The shepherds heard the story [C]proclaimed by [Bb]angels [C]bright, How [F]Christ,
the Lord of [C]glory [Bb]was born [C]on earth [F]this
night. [Dm]To Bethlehem they [C]sped
and in the manger [C]found [Bb]Him, As [C]angel heralds [F]said.

Verse 4
[F]This Flow'r, whose fragrance tender [C]with [Bb]sweetness [C]fills the [F]air,
Dispels with glorious [C]splendor [Bb]the darkness [C]everywhere; True [F]Man,
yet [Dm]very God, [C]from
sin and death He [C]saves [Bb]us, And [C]lightens ev'ry [F]load.

Verse 5
[F]O Savior, Child of Mary, [C]who felt [Bb]our [C]human woe, [F]O
Savior, King of [C]glory, who [Bb]dost our [C]weakness know; [F]Bring
us [Dm]at length we [C]pray,
to the bright courts [C]of Heaven, [Bb]And to [C]the endless [F]day!
